      "interpretation": "Total, short-chain, and long-chain PFAS removal efficiencies by CPS and ZIPGEM were evaluated under varying field water quality (pH, ammonia, season). PFAS removal was used as the performance metric for the filtration cells.",
      "reported_term": "PFAS removal efficiency"
    },
    {
      "endpoint_type": "removal",
      "evidence_locator_ids": [
        "S09-E01",
        "S09-E02",
        "S09-E05"
      ],
      "interpretation": "Spearman and PCA analyses showed that total PFAS removal efficiency is negatively associated with influent and effluent ammonia concentrations; PFOS removal appeared most impacted, with PFAS removal loading in a quadrant opposite to ammonia in PCA space, indicating ammonia-related competition or charge effects on adsorption.",
      "reported_term": "Effect of ammonia on PFAS removal efficiency"
    },
    {
      "endpoint_type": "removal",
      "evidence_locator_ids": [
        "S09-E01",
        "S09-E07"
      ],
      "interpretation": "Short- and long-chain PFAS removal efficiencies were negatively correlated with proton concentration (i.e., decreased when pH decreased). Short-chain PFAS showed stronger negative correlation, consistent with electrostatic adsorption sensitivity to surface protonation and charge neutralization, whereas long-chain PFAS, dominated by hydrophobic interactions, were less affected.",
      "reported_term": "Effect of pH (proton concentration) on PFAS removal"
    },
    {
      "endpoint_type": "other",
      "evidence_locator_ids": [
        "S09-E05",
        "S09-E06",
        "S09-E07"
      ],
      "interpretation": "Authors infer that proton exchange among DOC, ammonium ions, and media components, driven by changes in influent/effluent pH, affects media surface charge and hence PFAS removal. Increased NH4+ in the media over time, formation of ammonium at lower pH, and protonation/deprotonation of media and natural organic matter are proposed to influence PFAS adsorption via competition, charge neutralization, and changes in cation-bridging capacity.",
      "reported_term": "Water-matrix interaction mechanisms (ammonia, DOM, protons)"
    }
